---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro'
import Steps from '../../components/Steps.astro'
import { VaultReplica } from '../../components/app/Replicas'
---

<Guide
  slug="credentials"
  title="Saving a credential"
  description="Add a login to the vault, reveal it when you need it, and copy it without leaving a trail in Windows clipboard history."
  lede="The Vault holds logins. This is the part of Sanctum that behaves like a password manager, with one difference worth knowing about in how copying works."
>
  <h2>Adding one</h2>
  <Steps steps={[
    'Open <strong>Vault</strong> in the sidebar and click the <strong>+</strong> button on the right of the toolbar.',
    'Fill in the name. It is the only field that matters &mdash; an entry you cannot identify later is worse than an untidy one.',
    'Add the username, URL, notes and tags as you need them. All of them are optional.',
    'Type the password, or click <strong>Generate</strong> for one that clears the strength floor.',
    'Pick a folder if you group by client. Save.',
  ]} />

  <VaultReplica />

  <Callout kind="note" title="What gets encrypted, and what does not">
    <p>
      The name, username, password, URL, notes and tags are all encrypted individually before they
      touch disk. The folder it belongs to and the times it was created and changed stay readable.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is a deliberate trade rather than an oversight. Someone holding your vault file without
      the passphrase can count how many credentials exist and see when you added them. They cannot
      read a single name, username or password. Encrypting the timestamps too would mean decrypting
      every record just to sort a list.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Getting it back out</h2>
  <p>
    Each row has an eye and a copy control. The eye reveals one password at a time; the others stay
    masked. Nothing is revealed by default, and the list view never loads passwords at all &mdash;
    they are fetched only when you ask for that specific one.
  </p>

  <h3>Copying is not an ordinary copy</h3>
  <p>
    Windows keeps a clipboard history, and if you have it enabled it may sync that history to your
    Microsoft account. A password manager that ignores this quietly leaks every password you copy
    into a list that outlives the copy.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sanctum marks each copy with the Windows formats that ask history, cloud sync and monitoring to
    leave it alone, then clears the clipboard after thirty seconds &mdash; but only if the contents
    are still what it put there, so it never wipes something you copied afterwards.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="When Windows keeps a copy anyway">
    <p>
      Those exclusion formats are a request, not a guarantee, and some clipboard managers ignore
      them. When Sanctum cannot confirm the exclusion took effect, it tells you rather than staying
      quiet about it.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can check for yourself: press <kbd>Win</kbd> + <kbd>V</kbd> after copying. A password
      that appears there was not excluded.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Finding things later</h2>
  <ul class="plainList">
    <li><strong>Search</strong> matches names, usernames, URLs and tags. It never matches passwords &mdash; doing so would mean holding every password in memory to compare against.</li>
    <li><strong>The tag filter</strong> narrows to one tag, and search narrows within it.</li>
    <li><strong>Ctrl+K</strong> opens a palette that searches every module at once. It surfaces titles, never secrets.</li>
    <li><strong>Favourites</strong> cut across every module and are the fastest way back to what you touch daily.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Folders are per module</h2>
  <p>
    A folder belongs to one module. A folder called <em>Acme</em> in the Vault is a different thing
    from one called <em>Acme</em> in Notes or Env Files, and a credential cannot be filed into a
    notes folder.
  </p>
  <p>
    That keeps each module's folder list clean, at the cost of creating the same client name in
    each module you use for them. Worth knowing before you set up twelve clients.
  </p>

  <h2>Generating passwords</h2>
  <p>
    The generator is its own tool in the sidebar, and also sits inside the credential form. It
    produces either random strings, with length and character classes you choose, or passphrases of
    several words. Both are scored with the same strength meter used at setup, so you can see what
    you are getting rather than trusting the length alone.
  </p>
</Guide>
